$DIR/main.p:6:6: a function literal that captures variables cannot be started with go: out lives in the starting frame, which may be gone before the puroutine runs (spec section 4.7); pass it as an argument instead: go func(out chan int) {...}(out)
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it reaches
    them in that function's stack frame. A go statement may start it after that
    function has returned, when the frame is gone.
    Pass the values the literal needs as arguments: they are copied when the go
    statement runs.
    Go moves captured variables to the heap. Plutonium never allocates behind
    the program's back, so captured variables stay in their frame (spec section
    4.5).
    Pass the values as arguments:
        for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
            go func(i int, out chan int) {
                out <- i * i
            }(i, out)
        }
    (see "go starts a puroutine" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2580" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:10:2: go with the built-in println is not supported; start a function that calls it
    A go statement cannot start a built-in function such as println or close
    directly. Wrap it in a function and start that.
    (see "go starts a puroutine" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2581" for a longer explanation)
